<title>BELGIUM: E.Europe seen importing 1.1 mln tns grain in 2005.</title>
<headline>E.Europe seen importing 1.1 mln tns grain in 2005.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-04-24</dateline>
<text>
<p>A group of 10 central and east European countries seeking to join the European Union will become net importers of 1.1 million tonnes of grain in 2005, European Commission projections showed.</p>
<p>For the same year, the current 15 EU member states are projected to have a 36 million tonne exportable surplus.</p>
<p>&quot;The east Europeans will not be an important factor (in the EU's growing grain surplus) during this period,&quot; a Commission official said, adding the EU budgetary cost would be less than expected.</p>
<p>Crop recovery after the disruption following the collapse of communism at the end of the 1980s was slower than expected, the Commission's agriculture department said.</p>
<p>Cereals production in the 10 prospective member countries is expected to rise to 83.7 million tonnes in 2005 from 72.6 million tonnes in 1994.</p>
<p>Domestic use is expected to grow to 84.8 million tonnes, from 72.7 million tonnes over the same period. Domestic demand from pig and poultry producers for cereal feed has been stronger than expected and reduced exportable supplies.</p>
<p>The projections assume that all 10 countries join the EU in 2002 and respect set-aside rates under the Common Agricultural Policy in its present form.</p>
<p>The 10 prospective members are Bulgaria, Czech republic, Estonia, Hungary, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland, Romania, Slovak republic and Slovenia.</p>
<p>-Brussels Bureau +322 287 6830</p>
